     "The course maps the properties to four design consequences that recur throughout the exam. **Non-determinism**: the same input can produce different outputs across runs. This is why evaluation frameworks exist; you cannot certify behavior you observed only once. It feeds evaluation design.",
     "**Context as a finite resource**: the context window is a hard edge with a fixed token budget. What you put in it, in what order, and what you leave out are design decisions that affect both what the model can work with and what each call costs. It feeds model and context strategy.",
     "**Confidence is not validity**: Claude can deliver a wrong answer in the same fluent, assured tone it uses for a right one. That is why human-in-the-loop placement and verification are architectural choices, not afterthoughts. It feeds responsible deployment. **Knowledge and capability boundaries**: the model is reliable on topics that are common, recent and consistent in its training data and unreliable on topics that are rare, private or fast-changing. For the unreliable zone, web search, retrieval, tools and MCP make an external system the source of truth. It feeds reference architectures and RAG.",
