    "id": "pf1-case-deterministic-drift",
    "part": "p1b",
    "h": "Case study: when the deterministic check quietly drifted",
    "p": [
     "What happened: a partner had a rule that any claim over £5,000 needs a senior adjuster, enforced by an SQL check against the claims table. In scoping, the architect proposed letting Claude extract the amount and route accordingly, 'so it's one step instead of reaching out to a separate system'. The partner agreed. Three months later, of 14,000 claims processed, 41 had routed incorrectly.",
     "Why: all 41 had the amount buried in prose, such as 'damages estimated around five thousand pounds'. The model treated 'around five thousand' as a loose estimate rather than a figure that should trigger senior review. The threshold never changed; what enforced it did. A rule that must be right every time was handed to a system that is right most of the time, and the gap is where the 41 misroutes lived.",
     "Compounding gaps: the team never built test cases for routing, because they treated it as something the model 'would just handle' rather than a rule the business counted on. And the misroutes were caught by an audit, not by monitoring; logging that would have caught a broken SQL check does not record the choices a model makes inside a single request.",
     "The fix: Claude extracts the amount (including amounts written in words, flagged with confidence) into a structured field; deterministic code applies the threshold; ambiguous or low-confidence extractions route to the senior queue or a human; and an eval set of messy amount phrasings guards the extraction. The lesson: a rule the business is counting on must be tested, watched and owned. Clean demo cases hide the cost of 'most of the time' until the audit."
    ],
    "why": "This is the archetypal 'deterministic rule in a probabilistic system' trap. On the exam, the option that keeps the threshold in code and uses Claude only for extraction is correct.",
