     "What happened: a regional bank wanted an 'operations assistant' for branch staff to look up customer balances, schedule appointments and answer policy questions. The proposed design: Claude Code on branch laptops with a per-branch CLAUDE.md for local conventions; MCP servers for the customer database, appointment system and policy corpus; and subagents running compliance checks on every interaction with a restricted toolset.",
     "Why it failed review, three mechanisms: (1) the entry point was chosen before the user was named; branch staff do not run terminals, so an engineering entry point was mismatched to an operational workflow. (2) MCP was carried forward from a prior project as a default; the reuse argument did not apply because no other Claude clients existed in the bank, so the protocol layer paid integration cost for nothing. (3) Compliance, the highest-consequence path, was assigned to subagents, which provide weaker deterministic guarantees than server-side code; the work that most needed code-level certainty ran furthest from it.",
     "The fix: a custom web application calling the API directly, authenticated through the bank's SSO and shaped for a banking workflow; compliance in deterministic server-side code with explicit guarantees; tool calls audited at the server boundary; live balances via direct tool calls to the system of record.",
     "The lesson: entry-point choice follows the user and the work. Reaching for Claude Code or MCP because the last project used them is paying for capabilities the partner does not need."
