    "id": "pf2-batch-processing",
    "part": "p2a",
    "h": "Message Batches: a cost lever for work that can wait",
    "p": [
     "Not every call is on a user-facing path. For offline work — large-scale evaluations, content moderation backlogs, bulk summarization or generation — the Message Batches API processes many Messages requests asynchronously at 50% of standard prices. Most batches finish within an hour, and results are available when all requests complete or after 24 hours, whichever comes first; requests not processed in 24 hours expire.",
     "Batching changes the design: each request carries a unique `custom_id` because results can return in any order; you poll for completion and retrieve results (available for 29 days). It is not suitable for interactive or latency-sensitive paths, and it does not support streaming.",
     "Batch and caching discounts stack. Because batches can run longer than five minutes, the docs recommend the 1-hour cache TTL for batches sharing context, plus identical `cache_control` blocks in every request; hits are best-effort because requests run concurrently. Running your eval suite (see [[pf2-eval-gate]]) through batches is a common way to make frequent re-evaluation affordable."
    ],
    "why": "When a scenario says 'overnight', 'backlog' or 'no user waiting', the cost answer is often batching rather than downgrading the model — quality stays the same and price halves.",
    "why2": "Proposing batches for a real-time chat path, or forgetting that results must be matched by custom_id, not by order.",
    "deep": "Limits: up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB per batch; `custom_id` must match ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$. Cache hit rates in batches typically range 30% to 98%. (Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing)",
    "links": [
     {
      "to": "pf2-cache-economics",
      "dir": "related",
      "why": "Discounts stack with caching"
     }
    ]
