    "id": "pf2-claude-techniques",
    "part": "p2e",
    "h": "Claude-specific prompting practices: structure, long context, chaining",
    "p": [
     "**Be clear, direct and motivated.** Say exactly what you want, use numbered steps when order matters, and explain why a rule exists — Claude generalizes from the reason. Say what to do rather than what not to do ('write in flowing prose paragraphs' beats 'don't use markdown'), and match your prompt's own style to the output you want. Phrase actions as actions: 'change this function' produces edits, 'can you suggest changes' produces suggestions.",
     "**Use XML tags** such as `<instructions>`, `<context>`, `<input>` and `<example>` to separate parts of the prompt, with consistent, descriptive names and nesting for hierarchy (for example `<documents>` containing indexed `<document>` blocks). Tags make templates safer too: slot content is clearly delimited data. **Give a role** in the system prompt; one sentence of role framing helps.",
     "**Long-context prompts (20k+ tokens):** put long documents at the top and the query at the end — Anthropic reports this can improve response quality by up to 30% in tests, especially with complex multi-document inputs. Wrap each document with its source metadata, and ask Claude to extract relevant quotes before doing the task to ground the answer. Note how well this aligns with caching: documents first, query last is also static-before-dynamic.",
     "**Prompt chaining** splits work into separate calls when you need to inspect, log, evaluate or branch on intermediate outputs — the common pattern is draft, review against criteria, refine. With adaptive thinking and subagents, much multi-step reasoning happens inside one call, so chain when you need the control, not by habit. **Prefill** (starting the assistant's turn for it) is deprecated on newer models — prefilling the last assistant turn returns a 400 error — so control format with instructions, XML format indicators or structured outputs instead."
    ],
