    "analogy": "Think of a restaurant: the entry point is the dining room (who sits where), the build-time interface is the kitchen's recipes and equipment, and the delivery route is which building the kitchen is in. Changing buildings does not change the menu, and redecorating the dining room does not move the kitchen.",
    "why": "Exam scenarios frequently hide a layer confusion inside a plausible proposal. If you can label each component by layer, you can isolate exactly which decision is wrong instead of rejecting the whole design.",
    "why2": "Assuming that because 'it is all Claude' (the same model family sits under every entry point), any entry point is interchangeable. The model is shared; the wrapper, its audience, and its controls are not.",
    "deep": "The official Agent SDK overview draws the same distinctions at the build-time layer: the Agent SDK embeds Claude Code's agent loop as a library in a process you operate; the Claude Code CLI is the interactive terminal interface; the Client SDK gives direct API access where you write the tool loop yourself (or use the beta tool runner); and Managed Agents has Anthropic host the agent harness, configured through the Claude API. (Source: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview)",
