    "code": "\"mcp_servers\": [{\"type\": \"url\", \"url\": \"https://mcp.example.com/mcp\", \"name\": \"tickets\", \"authorization_token\": \"<per-user OAuth token>\"}],\n\"tools\": [{\n  \"type\": \"mcp_toolset\",\n  \"mcp_server_name\": \"tickets\",\n  \"default_config\": {\"enabled\": false},\n  \"configs\": {\"tickets_search\": {\"enabled\": true}, \"tickets_get\": {\"enabled\": true}}\n}]",
    "codeExplain": "An allowlist: every tool on the server is disabled by default and only two read tools are enabled. The token is obtained by your application per user, not shared across users.",
    "why": "Platform availability and ZDR status of the MCP connector are exactly the kind of detail that decides an exam question about a regulated deployment on Bedrock or Vertex.",
    "why2": "Pointing the MCP connector at a localhost or stdio server. It only reaches publicly exposed HTTPS servers.",
    "deep": "MCP connector beta header per the docs: `mcp-client-2025-11-20` (a newer `mcp-client-2026-09-15` adds tool-list pinning on the Claude API). Tool precedence is per-tool `configs`, then `default_config`, then system defaults; every server must be referenced by exactly one toolset. Claude Code scope precedence is local over project over user over plugin-provided over claude.ai connectors, with whole entries used rather than merged. (Sources: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector, https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp)",
