    "id": "pf3-integration-layers",
    "part": "p3d",
    "h": "The enterprise integration layers: compliance first, then identity, authorization, data, observability",
    "p": [
     "Sizing tells you what the system must do and whether it can do it within constraints. Integration patterns tell you how it connects to the enterprise stack. The course breaks that connection into layers, each with a decision that belongs to the Architect rather than to the implementation team. **Compliance comes first**: regulatory and policy constraints eliminate delivery routes and entry points before any other decision is made, and the remaining layers operate within whatever options survive that filter.",
     "Work through the constraints in order: identify the governing regulation or policy; determine which entry points and routes are still available; choose the integration pattern that fits; and document the identity, data-handling, and observability requirements that follow. Skipping a step risks a system that works technically but fails the next legal or security review, at which point the redesign costs the time already spent plus a new architecture.",
     "Each layer has a characteristic failure. Identity passed as a raw field in the user message is manipulable, so Claude cannot reliably scope responses to what the user may see. An integration that bypasses the underlying system's authorization model creates an unenforced path to data. A sensitive field placed in the prompt lands in plaintext in application request logs and surfaces at the next audit. An unlogged data path is invisible when something goes wrong."
    ],
