    "id": "pf4-eval-workflow",
    "part": "p4a",
    "h": "The eval workflow: from task definition to result",
    "p": [
     "A well-constructed eval workflow runs sequentially through five stages, and each stage produces an artifact consumed by the next. Skipping or blurring a stage degrades everything downstream: a vague task definition produces a vague eval, and an unrepresentative dataset makes even a perfect grader meaningless.",
     "Stages 3 and 4 are complementary rather than alternatives. Automated code checks handle the unambiguous behaviors (format, schema, lookups against authoritative data) cheaply on every item; a model-based judge handles behaviors that require interpretation (tone, reasoning quality, edge-case appropriateness). Most real suites use both on the same item.",
     "Stage 5 is where teams most often fool themselves. An aggregate score that rises can hide a fall on edge cases or adversarial inputs. Always read the per-category breakdown: a change that raises the mean while quietly degrading the hard categories has not made the system better, it has moved risk to where you look least."
    ],
