    "id": "pf4-eval-gate",
    "part": "p4a",
    "h": "Evals as the gate for every change, and multi-turn evals",
    "p": [
     "Every change to a production Claude system — model swap, prompt revision, context strategy change, retrieval configuration update — should run through the eval suite during development before it moves to production. It is the only reliable way to know whether the change improved the system rather than just changed it.",
     "The gate only works if the suite tracks the system. A document-summarization team revised its prompt but did not update its eval set; the suite passed every check. Two days after release, field reports showed multi-clause legal sentences being truncated in summaries. The eval set predated the prompt change and simply did not exercise the behavior that changed. Rule: when you change behavior, change the eval that measures it in the same pull request.",
     "Single-turn evals cannot tell you how the system holds up across a conversation. Multi-turn evals score a whole sequence of exchanges: does the system keep prior context straight, does it answer follow-ups without inventing details never said earlier, does quality hold as the conversation lengthens. Because the unit scored is a conversation, this category needs its own golden dataset of full transcripts with known-good responses at each turn, covering the follow-ups, topic shifts and lengths you will see in production."
    ],
    "why": "The highest-risk moment for a regression is when evals exist but are out of date — the green checkmark actively suppresses scrutiny. The exam expects you to keep the eval set current with the system it measures.",
    "why2": "Assuming a passing eval suite means a change is safe. Ask first whether the suite covers the behavior the change touched.",
    "deep": "The docs' example of a multi-turn criterion is context utilization graded by an LLM on an ordinal 1–5 scale across a set of multi-turn conversations. Model version changes belong in the gate too: Anthropic recommends testing thoroughly with replacement models well before a deprecated model's retirement date. (Sources: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests ; https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/model-deprecations)",
