    "id": "pf4-case-wrong-thing",
    "part": "p4a",
    "h": "Case study: the eval suite that measured the wrong thing",
    "p": [
     "What happened: a team built a contract review assistant for a professional services firm. They manually tested it against ten contracts they knew well, declared it ready and shipped. Two weeks later, a regression: on contracts with non-standard obligation structures, the model extracted obligations from the wrong section. An eval suite did exist — built at project start from the same ten development contracts. When the prompt later changed, the suite kept passing because its expected outputs still reflected the old prompt's behavior.",
     "Why: three decisions compounded. The dataset was built from convenient inputs rather than a representative sample, so it measured a different system from the one shipped. The eval set was not updated after the prompt changed, so stable scores reflected an absence of tests on what had changed. And manual spot-checks were treated as an eval substitute — spot-checks confirm that one known input gives one known output, but say nothing about unknown inputs.",
     "The fix: rebuild the golden set by sampling the actual contract population, with explicit categories for non-standard obligation structures and other rare layouts; tie eval updates to every prompt change; and gate releases on per-category results rather than spot-checks.",
     "The lesson: the suite was present but misconfigured in two ways — not representative and not current. Both are invisible until production exposes the gap, because the system looks healthy when tested only on inputs it was already optimized for. Teams can only test the inputs they thought of; production supplies the rest."
    ],
    "why": "This is the canonical 'false confidence' pattern the exam tests: an eval that exists, passes, and is wrong.",
    "why2": "Concluding 'the model is unreliable' and swapping models. The model was never tested on the failing input class; the eval was the defect.",
