    "id": "pf4-cost-model",
    "part": "p4b",
    "h": "Cost modeling at production volume: the token math",
    "p": [
     "Build the cost and latency model before finalizing the architecture. Three inputs: call volume (requests per day or month), token budget per request (input plus expected output), and model tier. From those you estimate monthly cost and compare it to the budget ceiling before any production code exists.",
     "The arithmetic: monthly input tokens = requests × input tokens per request; monthly output tokens = requests × output tokens per request; cost = input tokens × input rate + output tokens × output rate, with cached-prefix tokens priced at the cache-read rate and cache writes at the write rate. Output tokens are typically priced several times higher per token than input, so output length is a first-class cost lever, not an afterthought.",
     "The table uses the course's customer-service scenario (50,000 requests/month; 5,000-token stable system prompt; 300 input and 400 output tokens average) with illustrative rates of $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens, and the docs' standard cache-read multiplier of 0.1× input. The rates are placeholders for the arithmetic — use the current pricing page for real numbers. The pattern is what matters: the stable system prompt is 94% of input tokens, so caching it is the dominant lever.",
     "Add a volume multiplier for growth, peaks and retries (the course calculator uses 1.8×) and error bounds. Present projections as ranges with stated assumptions, never as a single point estimate."
    ],
