    "id": "pf4-reliability-controls",
    "part": "p4b",
    "h": "Reliability controls: retries, fallbacks, circuit breakers and timeouts",
    "p": [
     "Transient error recovery with exponential backoff: on a transient error (429 rate limit, 529 overloaded, 500, timeout) retry with progressively longer delays, plus jitter, honoring the retry-after header when present. Backoff prevents a retry storm from turning a brief hiccup into an outage. Cap attempts and total wait time based on what the use case can tolerate. The official SDKs already retry transient failures with exponential backoff (twice by default, configurable via max_retries), so configure rather than double-wrap.",
     "Fallback chains: if the primary model or endpoint is unavailable, route automatically to an alternative — a different model tier, a cached or templated response, or a queue for later processing — rather than surfacing an error. Fallbacks must be tested as part of the eval suite: a fallback model that has never been evaluated on your task is an untested system.",
     "Circuit breakers: measure the error rate on a downstream dependency and trip when it exceeds a threshold; while open, requests fail fast (or go straight to the fallback) instead of waiting on timeouts. This stops one degraded dependency from exhausting threads and taking down the wider system. Timeouts: set them deliberately; for long generations, stream or batch rather than holding a large non-streaming request open.",
     "Placement matters: retries belong close to the API call, circuit breakers at the service boundary, fallback chains in the orchestration layer. Put them in the wrong layer and you protect the wrong thing. Also know which errors not to retry: a 400 invalid request or 401/403 will fail identically; a 429 caused by hitting the usage tier's monthly spend cap carries no retry-after and keeps failing until access resumes."
    ],
    "table": {
     "headers": [
      "Status / type",
      "Meaning",
      "Correct handling"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "400 invalid_request_error",
       "Malformed or invalid request (also user-set spend limits)",
       "Fix the request; do not retry unchanged"
      ],
      [
       "401 / 403",
       "Auth or permission problem",
       "Fix credentials or permissions; alert"
      ],
      [
       "413 request_too_large",
       "Request exceeds byte limit",
       "Reduce payload, use Files API or split"
      ],
      [
       "429 rate_limit_error",
       "Rate limit, acceleration limit, or tier spend cap",
       "Back off per retry-after; ramp traffic gradually; spend-cap 429 won't recover by retrying"
      ],
      [
       "500 api_error",
       "Unexpected internal error",
       "Retry with exponential backoff; escalate with request-id if persistent"
      ],
      [
       "504 timeout_error",
       "Request timed out while processing",
       "Use streaming for long requests; retry"
      ],
      [
       "529 overloaded_error",
       "API temporarily overloaded across users",
       "Backoff, then fallback chain; circuit breaker if sustained"
      ]
     ]
    },
    "why": "Reliability questions test both the control and its layer. 'Add retries in the UI' or 'retry 400s' are typical wrong answers.",
    "why2": "Aggressive immediate retries with no backoff or cap. Under overload they multiply load and lengthen the outage.",
    "deep": "The errors page: 529 errors can occur during high traffic across all users; a sharp, sudden usage increase can trigger 429 acceleration limits, so ramp up gradually; consider streaming or batches for requests over 10 minutes; every response carries a request-id header to include in support requests; errors can also occur mid-stream after a 200 response, which standard handling does not catch. (Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/errors)",
