    "id": "pf4-ex-calculator",
    "part": "p4b",
    "h": "Worked exercise: the cost and reliability calculator",
    "p": [
     "Scenario: a customer service agent handles 50,000 requests a month. The system prompt is 5,000 tokens and stable; average user input is 300 tokens and output 400. Ceiling: $800/month. Target: p95 ≤ 3 seconds. The calculator lets you change model tier, prompt caching, max_tokens cap and a call-volume multiplier; its starting state read about $1,918/month and 4.1 s p95 — over both targets.",
     "Decision: once a configuration meets both targets, which lever did the most work? Model answer: turning on prompt caching. The 5,000-token prompt is identical across all 50,000 requests and makes up the overwhelming majority of input tokens; caching moves it to the cache-read rate and also improves time to first token on a long prefix. Using the illustrative arithmetic in [[pf4-cost-model]], caching takes the base volume from roughly $1,095 to $420, and even at the 1.8× multiplier the cached configuration lands near $756, under the ceiling.",
     "Why the distractors fail: switching to the most capable tier is the most expensive option and pushes cost further over the ceiling at this volume — 'most capable is safest' ignores the cost dimension of the acceptance criteria. Raising the max_tokens cap gives more headroom, not more quality; it raises worst-case cost and latency and helps neither target. Remaining latency headroom typically comes from a faster tier (validated by evals), shorter outputs, and streaming for perceived latency."
    ],
    "table": {
     "headers": [
      "Option",
      "Verdict",
      "Reason"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "A. Switch to the most capable tier",
       "Wrong",
       "Highest per-token cost; pushes over the ceiling; likely slower"
      ],
      [
       "B. Turn on prompt caching",
       "Correct",
       "Targets the dominant cost driver (5,000 stable tokens × 50,000 calls); improves TTFT"
      ],
      [
       "C. Raise max_tokens",
       "Wrong",
       "Raises cost and latency ceilings; doesn't improve quality or meet either target"
      ]
     ]
    },
    "why": "Calculator-style questions reward finding the dominant term in the token math, not picking the most 'powerful' option.",
