    "id": "pf5-guardrails-layers",
    "legacy": 0,
    "part": "p5a",
    "h": "Implementing guardrails and safety controls",
    "p": [
     "Effective guardrails operate at MULTIPLE layers, not just one: prompt-level instructions (advisory, probabilistic), programmatic hooks or prerequisite gates (deterministic, code-enforced), output-side filtering (a final check before content reaches a user), and access-level restrictions (least privilege on what a system can even attempt). No single layer is treated as sufficient alone — a genuinely robust design assumes any ONE layer might fail and backs it up with independent others.",
     "The full defense-in-depth stack for a Claude deployment has seven layers. Each answers a different question, and the key design move is making their failure modes independent: a jailbreak that defeats the prompt should still hit a deterministic authorization check; an output filter that misses a leak should still leave an audit trail that surfaces it.",
     "Anthropic's own guidance follows this shape. The jailbreak-mitigation guide recommends combining a lightweight harmlessness screen, input validation, prompt engineering, monitoring for repeat offenders and chained safeguards. Anthropic's containment engineering post makes the stronger point: enforce containment at the environment layer first (sandbox, least privilege, egress control, no credentials in the sandbox), then shape behavior at the model layer, because model-layer defenses are probabilistic.",
     "When you design or review, ask three questions of each layer: what does it catch, what does it miss, and what happens when it errors? The answers determine both the next layer you add and the failure direction you set."
    ],
