    "analogy": "Defense in depth is the Swiss-cheese model: every slice has holes, but the holes are in different places, so a hazard has to line up through all of them to cause harm.",
    "why": "Relying on a single guardrail layer — even a well-designed one — creates a single point of failure; layering independent controls is what makes the overall system resilient to any one control's individual failure.",
    "why2": "Adding several layers that all fail the same way, for example three prompt-based checks. Redundancy only helps when the layers' blind spots differ.",
    "deep": "Anthropic's containment post reports an internal red-team exercise in which the model was talked into making a data-exfiltration request in 24 of 25 attempts, and egress controls blocked it: the model layer alone was not enough. It also reports that per-action approval prompts were approved about 93% of the time, which is why enforced boundaries such as OS-level sandboxes are preferred over approval-by-prompt. (Sources: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/how-we-contain-claude ; https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/mitigate-jailbreaks)",
