    "id": "pf5-case-trained-refusals",
    "part": "p5a",
    "h": "Case study: trained refusals mistaken for a domain policy",
    "p": [
     "**What happened.** A team built an internal assistant for a partner whose data-handling policy forbade users from accessing records belonging to other business units. In review, Claude refused every harmful prompt the team tried, so they concluded cross-unit disclosure was covered by the same safety behavior and never built an authorization check. In production, a normal-looking, in-domain request asked for a forbidden record. Nothing about it looked harmful in general terms, so Claude answered.",
     "**Why it broke.** A domain policy was conflated with trained alignment. The rule the team believed was enforced did not exist anywhere: not in training (Claude never saw this partner's policy), not in a classifier, not in the system prompt, not in application code. Trained refusals cover broad harm, not deployment-specific rules. The red-team test was measuring the wrong thing: it proved Claude refuses harmful requests, not that it enforces a records boundary.",
     "**The fix.** Encode the rule in a layer you build, and in the right layer. Cross-unit access is an authorization question, so the control is a deterministic, identity-scoped data filter applied before retrieval (the model never receives another unit's records), plus a system-prompt statement of the policy and an audit log of access. A test suite should include in-domain, polite requests for forbidden records, not only overtly harmful prompts.",
     "**The lesson.** For every rule in the partner's policy, name the layer that enforces it. Claude cannot enforce what it was never given, and a rule that lives in no layer fails silently."
    ],
    "why": "This is the canonical boundary failure for the module: the exam will present a strong refusal record and tempt you to conclude a domain rule is covered.",
    "why2": "Responding to the incident by adding a sentence to the system prompt only. A prompt instruction is still talkable-out-of; data-access rules belong in deterministic authorization that keeps the records out of context entirely.",
