    "id": "pf5-quality-guardrails",
    "part": "p5b",
    "h": "Official guardrails for hallucination, consistency and prompt leak",
    "p": [
     "Not every risk is adversarial. Anthropic's guardrails documentation gives concrete, prompt-and-pipeline-level techniques for the quality failure modes that undermine trust in a deployment. For **hallucination**, the basic techniques are: explicitly allow Claude to say it does not know; for long documents, have Claude extract word-for-word quotes first and base its analysis only on them; and require citations, then have Claude find a supporting quote for each claim and retract any it cannot support. Advanced techniques include reviewing summarized thinking for faulty reasoning, best-of-N comparison (inconsistency across runs signals hallucination), iterative self-verification, and restricting Claude to the provided documents.",
     "For **output consistency**, the docs recommend Structured Outputs when you need guaranteed JSON-schema conformance, since prompt-based methods cannot guarantee it. Otherwise: specify the format precisely, constrain with examples (which work better than abstract instructions), ground answers in a fixed knowledge base with a fixed response format, chain prompts for complex tasks, and keep role-based applications in character by listing likely scenarios and expected responses. The docs note that prefilling the assistant turn is not supported on Claude 4.6 and later models; use Structured Outputs or system-prompt instructions there.",
     "For **prompt leak**, the docs open with caveats: use leak-resistant techniques only when necessary because they add complexity that can hurt performance, test thoroughly, and try output monitoring first. Strategies are separating context from queries via the system prompt, post-processing outputs with regex, keyword filters or a second LLM, leaving out proprietary details Claude does not need, and auditing regularly.",
     "Architecturally, notice that several of these are output-side controls (citation checks, leak regex, schema validation) and several are input-side (what you choose to put in context). They slot into the same layered path as the safety screens."
    ],
