    "why": "If an exam scenario includes RAG, email, browsing or MCP tools, expect the correct answer to address content arriving through those channels, not only the user's message.",
    "why2": "Pasting retrieved documents into the system prompt for 'authority'. That gives attacker-controlled text the highest-trust position in the context.",
    "deep": "Claude Code applies the same idea: for most fetches, WebFetch runs a separate model call over the page and Claude receives that call's answer instead of the raw page; network commands such as curl are not auto-approved by default; and the docs recommend VMs when interacting with external web services. Anthropic's browser research reports a 1% attack success rate for Claude Opus 4.5 against an adaptive best-of-N attacker and concludes no browser agent is immune. (Sources: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/mitigate-jailbreaks ; https://code.claude.com/docs/en/security ; https://www.anthropic.com/research/prompt-injection-defenses)",
