    "id": "pf5-stakes-variables",
    "part": "p5e",
    "h": "What sets the stakes of a decision: reversibility, cost and confidence",
    "p": [
     "Decision logging explains decisions after the fact; a **routing rule** stops the wrong ones from taking effect at all. Treat human review as a **budget**: reviewer attention is finite, so focus it on the highest-stakes items. Three variables set where it goes.",
     "**Reversibility** is how easily a wrong decision can be undone. **Cost of a wrong decision** is what the mistake causes if it goes through uncorrected. Together these set the **stakes**: a decision that is hard to reverse and expensive when wrong is high stakes regardless of how the system reached it. **Confidence** sits on top: the system's score for its own output. It does not change the stakes; it estimates how likely this output is to be wrong, which tells you how much of the volume needs a person. It is useful only to the extent it is **calibrated**, because a model can be confidently wrong, and confirming calibration is a task of its own.",
     "Combine them into one rule: **route to a person when a decision is low-confidence and either irreversible or high-cost; let confident, reversible, low-cost decisions through.** The hard cases are where variables disagree, such as high cost but easily reversed, or low confidence on something trivial. When they conflict, give more weight to cost and reversibility, because they determine the consequences of a mistake, and let confidence decide how much of the high-stakes volume can safely pass unreviewed.",
     "For the very highest-stakes actions, many teams also gate confident decisions (for example, all irreversible actions above a value threshold), because calibration is never perfect. The exercise in this part tests exactly that point: cost and reversibility are the deciding controls."
    ],
