    "id": "pf5-case-entry-point-not-compliance",
    "part": "p5f",
    "h": "Case study: passing entry-point selection felt like finishing compliance",
    "p": [
     "**The setup.** Passing the constraint pre-filter produces an immediate, visible result: the route is allowed, the entry point is cleared, the design can move. Proving each obligation is met produces nothing visible at design time, because the proof is an artifact you must build, attach to an owner and keep alive as configurations change. The moment that feels like a finish line and the moment a reviewer checks are often months apart.",
     "**What happened.** A team chose a compliant delivery route for a regulated workload and treated compliance as settled. They mapped obligations to controls once, in a design document, with no owners and no logging to show any control was operating. The **data-residency** control was correct on paper: processing pinned to the approved region. Months later a logging configuration change started writing request metadata to a store in a second region. Nothing flagged it, because no one owned residency and no artifact tracked where data landed. The gap surfaced at audit, when a reviewer asked for evidence that data stayed in-region and the team had a design document instead of a data-flow record.",
     "**Why it broke.** Surviving the pre-filter was mistaken for establishing compliance. A compliant route is a prerequisite, not proof. The residency control was real at design time and silently false in production, and nothing caught the difference because no artifact was watching it.",
     "**The fix and the lesson.** Give each obligation a control, a named owner and a living evidence artifact revalidated as the deployment changes: here, a data-flow record covering every store (including logs and metadata), configuration-change review for anything that writes data, and periodic queries showing in-region storage. An evidenced control set is the proof; the entry point is only the starting line."
    ],
    "why": "Residency and logging drift are classic exam scenarios; the tested insight is that controls need owners and evidence that are maintained over time.",
    "why2": "Re-running the original route analysis as the remedy. The route was fine; the missing pieces were ownership and an evidence artifact that would have caught drift.",
