    "id": "pf6-audience-framing",
    "legacy": 1,
    "part": "p6c",
    "h": "Communicating architectural decisions and tradeoffs",
    "p": [
     "A technical audience and a business/executive audience need genuinely different framings of the SAME underlying decision. A technical audience benefits from the mechanism (why this pattern, this model tier, this retrieval strategy). A business audience needs the CONSEQUENCE (what this means for cost, timeline, risk, and the outcome they actually care about) — not necessarily the underlying mechanism in full technical detail.",
     "The course puts it bluntly: technical precision is necessary but not sufficient. In architecture reviews a recommendation is often technically correct yet not understandable to the person who must approve it. The architect speaks in latency, logging depth, context size, retrieval pattern and deployment route; the executive is asking 'what happens to the business if we make the wrong choice?' At that point the room needs translation, not more detail.",
     "The course connects this to **Description**, one of the AI Fluency competencies (communicating effectively with AI), extended to the stakeholder side: tell the audience precisely what they need, in terms they understand, matched to their AI familiarity without sacrificing accuracy. Three practical rules: lead with the business outcome rather than the architecture; frame limitations honestly, because a security stakeholder trusts a system whose limits are clearly stated; and anticipate the peer-proof demand, since the stakeholder must justify the choice to others and should leave with that justification in hand."
    ],
    "why": "Explaining a model-tier choice to an executive in terms of token-level cost mechanics, rather than the bottom-line cost and quality tradeoff it actually produces, is a communication mismatch that undermines an otherwise sound technical decision.",
    "why2": "Simplifying for executives by dropping the downside. Translation changes the vocabulary, not the content: the risks and reversal cost must still be there.",
    "links": [
     {
      "to": "pf6-tradeoff-frame",
      "dir": "leads-to",
      "why": "the structure the translated message follows"
     }
    ]
