    "id": "pf6-demo-design",
    "part": "p6c",
    "h": "Designing a demo that advances the deal (Partner Track)",
    "p": [
     "This material is labeled **Partner Track: not tested by the Architect exam**, but it shows the same discipline applied to buyers. A weak demo can undo what discovery earned. A **capabilities demo** answers 'what can this system do?' and creates interest; a **scenario-specific demo** answers 'what does it do with my problem, my workflow and my constraints?' and creates confidence. Showing a polished generic feature tour after a strong discovery call can make the buyer wonder whether the team understood the problem.",
     "Four design decisions come before building any screens: scenario selection, limit placement, sales-team collaboration and data preparation. Of these, **limit placement** runs most against instinct. Decide in advance which one or two limitations the demo will name and frame them as intentional scope boundaries. A limitation the buyer discovers mid-demo drops confidence; one you name early reads as discipline. In healthcare, financial services and the public sector, a clear boundary signals rigor and a deflection signals risk. Prepare three answers: what is the limit, why does it exist, and what happens if the use case needs to go beyond it.",
     "For partners running parallel opportunities or handing off mid-cycle, the course recommends tracking demo design as a workstream on the go-to-market engagement map, with scenario, identified limitations, confirmed data source and sales-team sign-off as explicit deliverables."
    ],
