    "id": "pf6-case-observability",
    "part": "p6d",
    "h": "Case study: the observability stack that replaced the feedback loop",
    "p": [
     "**Setup.** An architect has built a rigorous observability stack: dashboards live, alerts configured, data flowing. It is easy and reasonable to conclude stakeholder feedback is covered. The course reconstructs ninety days of the alert log against the stakeholder review calendar.",
     "**What happened.** Weeks 1–3: eval score steady at baseline, latency and cost nominal, a launch review in week 1. Weeks 4–7: the eval score drifts down week over week, but the error rate is flat, so no hard alert fires and no review is scheduled. Weeks 8–12: the score is still declining and the stakeholder reports output is 'less useful lately'. The quarterly review finally surfaces it in week 12.",
     "**Why it broke.** Every metric needed was already being collected. What was missing was the decision layer: no governance rule mapped a slow quality drift to a review trigger, and the drift never crossed an error-rate threshold. A drift with no trigger is invisible until a human happens to notice. The stack was measuring the right thing and telling no one it mattered.",
     "**The fix and the lesson.** A dashboard collects and displays; a feedback loop maps each signal to a trigger, an owner and a required action. One governance row, a quality-drift trigger escalating to architect review by week 5 and to a stakeholder review once diagnosis confirmed the drift, would have caught it about seven weeks earlier. Build rows for slow drifts as well as hard failures."
    ],
