    "why": "Exam scenarios with a residency rule usually include a distractor that picks the right platform but leaves the default endpoint. The configuration step is part of the answer.",
    "why2": "Treating the platform name as the compliance control. A cloud provider route on a global endpoint, or the direct API on its global default, does not satisfy an in-region rule.",
    "deep": "Bedrock: global endpoints offer dynamic routing for availability and suit applications where residency is flexible; regional endpoints guarantee routing through specific geographies (US, EU, Japan, Asia-Pacific listed) and carry a pricing premium; prefixes such as global., us., eu. on inference-profile model IDs control routing. (Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/claude-on-amazon-bedrock) Direct API: inference_geo accepts 'us' or 'global' on supported models; it does not apply on Bedrock or Google Cloud, where region is set by the endpoint or inference profile, nor on Microsoft Foundry. (Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/manage-claude/data-residency)",
