    "id": "pf7-workflows",
    "legacy": 1,
    "part": "p7f",
    "h": "Improving developer workflows with AI-assisted tooling",
    "p": [
     "Beyond individual task completion, AI-assisted tooling can improve WORKFLOW-level efficiency: automating repetitive review checks, generating boilerplate consistently, or accelerating onboarding to unfamiliar codebases — identifying where in the team's actual daily workflow the friction genuinely is, and applying tooling there specifically, rather than adding AI assistance uniformly everywhere regardless of actual benefit.",
     "Claude Code's surfaces suit different workflow improvements. The terminal CLI and IDE extensions (VS Code, JetBrains) reduce friction during active development: exploring unfamiliar code, drafting and iterating with plan mode and checkpoints. Headless `claude -p`, routines and the GitHub Action automate repetitive tasks such as PR review, triage, reports and boilerplate generation. Code Review handles managed PR review. The Agent SDK embeds the agent loop in your own tools for complex, product-specific workflows. Choosing the right surface for the actual bottleneck is part of workflow optimization.",
     "Integration is also how a team's knowledge gets encoded. Conventions, review standards and repeated procedures that usually live in people's heads become CLAUDE.md entries, skills, hooks and plugins that Claude applies consistently, so good practice travels with the tooling instead of depending on who is in the room."
    ],
    "why": "The highest-value application of developer tooling is usually at a SPECIFIC, identified friction point in the existing workflow — spreading effort evenly across the whole workflow tends to produce less benefit than concentrating it where the real bottleneck is.",
