    "id": "pf7-case-merge",
    "part": "p7f",
    "h": "Case study: the merge nobody could explain",
    "p": [
     "What happened: a team adopted AI-assisted coding and shipped noticeably faster. Three weeks in, a generated change passed code review and tests and went to production, where it leaked data through an input it never validated.",
     "Why: in the post-incident review the author could not explain why the code handled that input the way it did. It looked plausible, the tests were green, and nobody asked the question the checklist would have forced: can the person merging this explain what it does and why? Speed had quietly replaced understanding — the judgment erosion diligence exists to catch.",
     "The fix and the lesson: adopt the four-dimension verification checklist as a merge gate, with the security item (input validation) and the human-understanding item explicitly signed off, and automate what can be automated (tests for untrusted inputs, security-focused review). The biggest risk named by the course is a team shipping output it no longer understands because it passed shallow checks, until an input nobody reasoned about reaches production."
    ],
    "why": "This case is the anchor example for diligence questions: green tests plus approval is not the same as understanding.",
    "why2": "Concluding that AI-generated code should be banned. The lesson is a review standard, not abandonment of the tool.",
