    "id": "pf7-ops-debugging",
    "legacy": 2,
    "part": "p7f",
    "h": "Supporting debugging and operational issue resolution",
    "p": [
     "When a Claude-powered system misbehaves in production, tracing the actual failure — was it a tool call, a model response, a configuration issue, an external dependency — requires the SAME structured, trace-based diagnostic approach used at the Foundations level, but applied at team/organizational scale: shared runbooks, consistent logging conventions, and a clear escalation path for issues beyond an individual developer's ability to resolve alone.",
     "At the operational level, teams track observability metrics specifically for Claude-powered systems: token efficiency (actual vs. budgeted spend), cost per transaction, model-level error rates (invalid tool calls, format violations, hallucinations), cache hit rate, and latency distributions (p50, p95, p99). An escalation scheme should define severity levels and owners — for example, Severity 1 goes immediately to the on-call senior engineer or architect, Severity 2 to the team lead, Severity 3 to the owning developer — so issues are routed to the right people and context is preserved across shifts and time zones. The exact tiers are an organizational choice; what matters is that they are written down.",
     "The Architect's role here is translation, not firefighting. The team usually reports a symptom (latency spiked, outputs degraded, a tool started failing); the Architect connects it to an architecture cause. Resolving one incident yourself is firefighting; teaching the team the symptom-to-cause path they can follow next time is support that lasts."
    ],
    "why": "An individual developer's ad hoc debugging approach doesn't scale to an organization — shared conventions for logging, tracing, and escalation are what let ANY team member effectively diagnose an issue, not just whoever happens to be most familiar with that particular system.",
    "why2": "Resolving every incident personally. It feels fast but guarantees the same issue comes back to you.",
