    "id": "pfs4-responsible",
    "part": "scenario4b",
    "h": "Worked walkthrough: the responsible-deployment layer for the contract reviewer",
    "p": [
     "The course's responsible-deployment stack applies directly to Option D. Layer 1, the boundary between trained behavior and the application layer: Claude's trained behavior handles broad harm classes, but it has never seen this firm's playbook, so every firm-specific rule (which clauses conflict, what the fallback language is, which deviations need a partner) belongs in the application layer, retrieved and versioned. Leave a domain rule to trained behavior and no downstream control can put it back.",
     "Layer 2, runtime controls on the request path: input screening (e.g., confirm the document came from iManage under the requesting user's matter permissions), output screening (schema validation from the evaluator pass; checks that a redline cites a real playbook rule), and tool-call authorization (the app, not the model, decides which iManage documents can be fetched). Set each to fail closed: if a screen errors, the clause is flagged for the associate rather than passed through unscreened.",
     "Layers 3 and 4, transparency and human-review routing, both depend on one decision log built once: contract and section ID, playbook version retrieved, model and settings, output, confidence, and the associate's accept/edit/reject action. Route by confidence, reversibility, and cost of a wrong answer, keyed to stakes rather than volume. Here every output already gets pre-action sign-off (nothing reaches a client unreviewed); the low-confidence flag adds attention routing inside that review. Skew can enter through the corpus (an outdated playbook), prompt framing, examples, or routing; per-clause retrieval of a versioned playbook addresses the first at its source.",
     "Layer 5, the control register: tie each obligation to a control, an owner, and an evidence artifact a reviewer would accept. A control with no evidence artifact is a claim you cannot prove operated."
    ],
