     "The network asks for the lowest-latency design. The tradeoff translation map frames any tradeoff in three elements. Gain (trim logging): faster perceived response and a smoother clinician workflow. Give-up: the per-interaction audit detail required to satisfy the health-privacy obligation. Reversal cost: once the system is built around the latency gain, restoring logging requires redesigning the interaction layer, and any gap period creates a compliance exposure that must be disclosed and remediated.",
     "The reversal cost is the element stakeholders most often miss and the one that settles the argument. A latency win can usually be pursued in other ways (asynchronous log writes, smaller prompts, caching a stable system prompt, streaming the draft), but an audit gap cannot be retroactively closed; the missing records simply do not exist.",
     "How to say it to the clinical lead: 'We can make it feel faster without dropping the audit record. Dropping the record would save some latency, but if we ever need it back we rebuild the interaction layer and have to disclose the gap. Here are the latency options that keep the record intact.'"
