    "p": [
     "The rule: use code-based evaluation wherever the expected answer is known or the check is deterministic, and reserve model-based (LLM judge) evaluation for interpretive judgments a function cannot encode. Four of the five dimensions are code-based. Only summary faithfulness needs an LLM judge.",
     "Safety is the instructive one because it needs two methods. 'No auto-deny' is binary: either a denial action was issued or it was not, so a code check on the action is exact and should be a hard gate. 'Summary faithfulness' asks whether the narrative represents the source claim without fabrication, an interpretive task, so it goes to an LLM judge with a clear rubric, calibrated against human-graded samples.",
     "Security is easy to over-engineer. Cross-claimant leakage can be checked deterministically by scanning each summary for identifiers that appear in any input claim other than the one being processed. A deterministic check is cheaper, faster, and more trustworthy than asking a model whether leakage occurred.",
     "Cost is also code-based: per-claim spend is computed from input tokens, output tokens, model tier, and whether prompt caching applied, then compared to the ceiling."
    ],
