    "id": "pfs5-rfp-evals-cost",
    "part": "scenario5b",
    "h": "Worked walkthrough: RFP assistant eval strategy and POC-to-production checklist",
    "p": [
     "Decision 1, eval strategy. Code-based: schema compliance on extracted citations (document name, page number, section). Model-based: relevance and appropriateness of the draft response to the RFP. Golden dataset: built from past RFPs with client names redacted, sourced from the firm's historical engagements. Client-confidential documents are excluded from the eval set unless the client has given explicit approval. The governance point: eval data is data use too, so confidentiality rules apply to the golden set, not just to production.",
     "Decision 2, cost model. 800 requests/day x 30 days = 24,000 requests/month. Input per request is about 5,200 tokens (system prompt ~2,000 for methodology overview and instructions, retrieved context ~3,000, query ~200); output about 600 tokens. That is roughly 125 million input tokens and 14 million output tokens per month, of which about 48 million input tokens are the stable system prompt, which is the portion prompt caching discounts. At Sonnet tier with caching on the stable prompt, the projection lands within range.",
     "The caveat the model answer insists on: these are flat averages that assume requests cluster near the mean. With a 5-to-80-page corpus, retrieved-context size is likely bimodal, and a tail of large-excerpt requests makes a flat average understate input cost. The 'within ceiling' verdict holds only under the assumed distribution and must be checked with a sensitivity analysis before anyone relies on it.",
     "Reliability: a fallback chain from Sonnet to Haiku for latency spikes. Most critical failure mode: retrieval-quality drift as documents are added. If new documents are indexed inconsistently, retrieval precision falls and draft quality falls with it, without any model change."
    ],
